https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/postgrad/part-iii/files/pastpapers/2009/Paper65.pdf

= 1
{parent=Paper 65}
{scope}

= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $G$ be the remaining gas <mass>, $S$ the formed stellar <mass>, and $G_0$ the initial gas <mass>; initially $S=0$ and the <gas-phase metallicity> is zero. Take the <stellar yield> $y$ and the <mass-loading factor> $\nu$ to be constant, with homogeneous mixing and no <galactic gas inflow>. With the specified zero bulk return, <mass conservation> gives
$$
dG=-(1+\nu)\,dS,\qquad G=G_0-(1+\nu)S.
$$
New metals add $y\,dS$, while <star formation> and the <galactic outflow> remove metals at the existing <gas-phase metallicity> $Z$. Thus
$$
d(GZ)=y\,dS-Z(1+\nu)\,dS,
\qquad G\,dZ=y\,dS=-\frac{y}{1+\nu}\,dG.
$$
The <leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution> therefore gives
$$
Z=\frac{y}{1+\nu}\ln\frac{G_0}{G}.
$$
For the usual <gas fraction of a galaxy>, $\mu=G/(G+S)$, the denominator is the baryonic <mass> still in the system, which decreases under the <galactic outflow>. Since $G_0=G+(1+\nu)S$, we have $G_0/G=1+(1+\nu)(1-\mu)/\mu$. **In the remaining-mass convention,**
$$
\boxed{Z(\mu)=\frac{y}{1+\nu}\ln\left[\frac{1+\nu(1-\mu)}{\mu}\right].}
$$
If instead $\mu$ denotes $G/G_0$ relative to the fixed initial reservoir, the same calculation gives $\boxed{Z=y\ln(1/\mu)/(1+\nu)}$. Stating the denominator matters: these two <gas fraction of a galaxy> conventions coincide for the <closed-box model of galactic chemical evolution>, recovered at $\nu=0$, but differ when gas escapes. Neglecting bulk return while retaining a nonzero <stellar yield> is the intended trace-metal approximation.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A star inherits the <gas-phase metallicity> at its birth. Set $y_e=y/(1+\nu)$; the preceding calculation gives $G(Z)=G_0e^{-Z/y_e}$ and $dS/dZ=G(Z)/y$. If the <initial mass function> is fixed, let $\kappa$ be the number of the counted stars per unit formed stellar <mass>; this includes any restriction to surviving long-lived tracers. The <metallicity distribution in a leaky box> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{dN}{dZ}=\frac{\kappa G_0}{y}\exp\left[-\frac{(1+\nu)Z}{y}\right].}
$$
The total eventual formed stellar <mass> is $G_0/(1+\nu)$, so $N_\infty=\kappa G_0/(1+\nu)$ and the equivalent normalized <metallicity distribution function> is
$$
\boxed{\frac1{N_\infty}\frac{dN}{dZ}=\frac{1+\nu}{y}e^{-(1+\nu)Z/y}.}
$$
If $N(Z)$ means a cumulative count rather than a count per <stellar metallicity> interval, <integration> yields
$$
\boxed{N(<Z)=N_\infty\left[1-e^{-(1+\nu)Z/y}\right].}
$$
At a finite observation time these expressions end at the current <gas-phase metallicity> $Z_f$ and the normalized distribution divides by $1-e^{-Z_f/y_e}$. A distribution per logarithmic <stellar metallicity> instead multiplies $dN/dZ$ by $(\ln10)Z$; it is not the same shape as the distribution per linear <stellar metallicity>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

All gas being used or expelled means $G\to0$, so $Z\to\infty$ in the formal <leaky-box model of galactic chemical evolution> and the final stellar <mass> is $S_\infty=G_0/(1+\nu)$. Weight the <stellar metallicity> by formed stellar <mass>, using $dS/dZ=(G_0/y)e^{-Z/y_e}$:
$$
\langle Z\rangle_*=
\frac{\int_0^\infty Z\,(G_0/y)e^{-Z/y_e}\,dZ}{G_0/(1+\nu)}
=\frac{(G_0/y)y_e^2}{G_0/(1+\nu)}.
$$
Here $\int_0^\infty u e^{-u}du=1$, by <integration by parts>. **The final mass-weighted mean is**
$$
\boxed{\langle Z\rangle_*=\frac{y}{1+\nu}.}
$$
As a metal-budget check, newly produced metal <mass> is $yS_\infty$; stars retain $\int Z\,dS=yG_0/(1+\nu)^2$, while the <galactic outflow> carries $\nu\int Z\,dS$. Their sum is exactly $yS_\infty$. An observed luminosity-weighted <stellar metallicity> need not equal this mass-weighted mean.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Assume the <stellar yield> is common to the compared <stellar populations> and compare their mass-weighted <stellar metallicities>. Inverting the <metallicity distribution in a leaky box> result gives
$$
\boxed{\nu=\frac{y}{\langle Z\rangle_*}-1.}
$$
For example, with $y=Z_\odot$, a solar-metallicity massive <elliptical galaxy> requires $\nu\simeq0$, whereas $\langle Z\rangle_*<0.01Z_\odot$ in a <dwarf galaxy> requires $\nu>99$. A supersolar <stellar metallicity> requires a correspondingly supersolar <stellar yield>: outflow alone cannot raise $\langle Z\rangle_*$ above $y$. If $y=2Z_\odot$, a massive <elliptical galaxy> with $\langle Z\rangle_*$ between $Z_\odot$ and $2Z_\odot$ has $0\le\nu\le1$, while the same low-metallicity <dwarf galaxy> needs $\nu>199$.

**For a yield of order solar, the required mass loading runs from order zero or unity in massive spheroids to order one hundred or more in faint dwarfs.** Without a specified <stellar yield>, the robust comparison is
$$
\frac{1+\nu_{\rm dwarf}}{1+\nu_{\rm giant}}
=\frac{\langle Z\rangle_{*,\rm giant}}{\langle Z\rangle_{*,\rm dwarf}}
\gtrsim100.
$$
This explains the sense of the <galaxy mass--metallicity relation>; <galactic gas inflow>, incomplete depletion, and changes in the <initial mass function> can also change observed <stellar metallicity>.

= v
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The <escape-speed scaling of wind mass loading> makes the proposed trend dynamically plausible: the shallower <Newtonian gravitational potential> of a <dwarf galaxy> lets <stellar feedback> expel more gas per unit stellar <mass>. If coupled feedback supplies specific <energy> $e_*$, escape requires $\nu v_{\rm esc}^2/2\lesssim e_*$; fixed coupling suggests $\nu\propto v_{\rm esc}^{-2}$ for an <energy-driven outflow>. Fixed specific momentum $p_*$ instead gives $\nu v_{\rm esc}\lesssim p_*$, or $\nu\propto v_{\rm esc}^{-1}$ for a <momentum-driven outflow>.

Since <escape velocity> has $v_{\rm esc}^2\sim GM/R$, galaxies whose characteristic radius grows more slowly than their gravitating <mass> have increasing binding energy per unit <mass> with increasing <mass>. For $R\propto M^a$ with $a<1$, the <energy-driven outflow> estimate gives $\nu\propto M^{a-1}$. Equivalently, greater <velocity dispersion> in massive spheroids implies greater escape energy and more effective retention of metals.

For scale, one <core-collapse supernova> of $10^{51}\,\mathrm{erg}$ per $100M_\odot$ formed supplies about $5\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{cm^2\,s^{-2}}$ before coupling losses. At ten percent coupling the energetic upper estimate $2e_*/v_{\rm esc}^2$ is about $110$ for $v_{\rm esc}=30\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$, but about $0.4$ for $500\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$. **Efficient loss from shallow potentials and retention in deep potentials can span the required range.** These are illustrative energetics rather than a unique prediction: cooling, entrainment, fallback and the actual <initial mass function> determine the escaping <mass-loading factor>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 65}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use a steady, isotropic, self-gravitating <dark-matter halo>, with $V$ its constant one-dimensional <velocity dispersion>. The <spherical Jeans equation> and spherical <mass conservation> read
$$
V^2\frac{d\rho}{dr}=-\rho\frac{GM(r)}{r^2},\qquad
\frac{dM}{dr}=4\pi r^2\rho.
$$
For the scale-free outer region, put $\rho=A r^{-p}$. Its enclosed <mass> scales as $r^{3-p}$, so $GM(r)/r$ is constant only if $p=2$; the <spherical Jeans equation> then gives $2V^2=GM(r)/r=4\pi GA$. Thus the <singular isothermal sphere> has
$$
\boxed{\rho(r)=\frac{V^2}{2\pi Gr^2},\qquad M(<r)=\frac{2V^2r}{G}.}
$$
Its <circular speed> is $v_c=\sqrt2V$. If a speed denoted $V$ were instead the <circular speed>, the coefficient would be $V^2/(4\pi G)$, but the printed variable is a <velocity dispersion>.

The assumptions matter. A constant <velocity dispersion> and spherical shape alone do not specify a unique central <mass density> or orbital distribution. Even with isotropy, the <spherical Jeans equation> integrates to $\rho=\rho_0\exp[-(\Phi-\Phi_0)/V^2]$, and the self-gravitating <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> gives
$$
\frac1{r^2}\frac{d}{dr}\left(r^2\frac{d\ln\rho}{dr}\right)
=-\frac{4\pi G}{V^2}\rho.
$$
There are finite-central-density isothermal solutions as well as the singular one; the $r^{-2}$ expression is the intended outer scale-free halo approximation. A finite halo must also end or steepen at large radius, since the untruncated <singular isothermal sphere> has infinite total <mass>.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $m_\chi$ be the <dark matter> particle <mass>, so its number density is $n_\chi=\rho/m_\chi$. Interpret $\epsilon$ as total emitted power per particle, and assume isotropic optically thin <dark-matter decay radiation>; volume emissivity is $j_d=\epsilon\rho/m_\chi$. At projected radius $R$, a line-of-sight coordinate $z$ has $r^2=R^2+z^2$. Writing $A=V^2/(2\pi G)$, the <surface brightness> is
$$
I_d(R)=\frac1{4\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty j_d\,dz
=\frac{\epsilon A}{4\pi m_\chi}
\int_{-\infty}^\infty\frac{dz}{R^2+z^2}
=\frac{\epsilon A}{4m_\chi R}.
$$
Here $z=R\tan\theta$ turns the <integral> into $\pi/R$. **For the isothermal outer halo,**
$$
\boxed{I_d(R)=\frac{\epsilon V^2}{8\pi Gm_\chi R}\propto R^{-1}.}
$$
If emissivity was defined per unit solid angle, the factor $1/(4\pi)$ is already included in $\epsilon$; this only changes the normalization. The radial law holds between any core and outer truncation. For example, a phenomenological cored profile $\rho=A/(r^2+r_c^2)$ gives $I_d=\epsilon A/[4m_\chi\sqrt{R^2+r_c^2}]$: finite central <surface brightness>, but the same $R^{-1}$ outer law. This cored interpolation is not being asserted to be the exact isotropic constant-dispersion equilibrium.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A two-body annihilation requires two nearby particles, so the <dark-matter annihilation radiation> has emissivity proportional to the square of the <mass density>. With a spatially constant rate coefficient, write
$$
j_a=C_a\rho^2,\qquad
C_a=\frac{E_a\langle\sigma v_{\rm rel}\rangle}{2m_\chi^2}
$$
for identical self-conjugate particles, where $E_a$ is the radiated <energy> per annihilation; the factor one-half counts unordered pairs. Particle and antiparticle populations instead require their respective number densities, without that identical-pair factor. For the <singular isothermal sphere>, the <surface brightness> becomes
$$
I_a(R)=\frac{C_aA^2}{4\pi}\int_{-\infty}^\infty
\frac{dz}{(R^2+z^2)^2}.
$$
The substitution $z=R\tan\theta$ gives $R^{-3}\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos^2\theta\,d\theta=\pi/(2R^3)$. Hence **annihilation gives the steeper projected profile**
$$
\boxed{I_a(R)=\frac{C_aA^2}{8R^3}
=\frac{C_aV^4}{32\pi^2G^2R^3}\propto R^{-3}.}
$$
For the illustrative cored profile used above, $I_a=C_aA^2/[8(R^2+r_c^2)^{3/2}]$. These expressions assume a smooth <dark-matter halo>, negligible absorption and constant $C_a$; unresolved density clumps or a velocity-dependent annihilation coefficient change the projected shape. The <projection of a spherical power-law emissivity> summarizes why three-dimensional powers $r^{-2}$ and $r^{-4}$ produce $R^{-1}$ and $R^{-3}$.

= iv
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Measure the logarithmic <surface brightness> slope in resolved radial annuli after accounting for instrumental blurring and foreground emission. Between a halo core and its outer boundary, <dark-matter decay radiation> predicts $d\ln I/d\ln R=-1$, whereas <dark-matter annihilation radiation> predicts $-3$; doubling radius therefore reduces the respective <surface brightness> by factors $2$ and $8$. These are quantitative templates to compare with independently mapped stellar and gaseous components.

An <exponential galactic disk> has $I_*(R)\propto e^{-R/R_d}$, so $d\ln I_*/d\ln R=-R/R_d$ and $I_*(2R)/I_*(R)=e^{-R/R_d}$. At $R=5R_d$ the latter is about $0.0067$, much faster decline than either halo template, and the projected emission should also be flattened along the <galactic disk>. A <galactic bulge> described by a <Sérsic profile> has $d\ln I_*/d\ln R=-(b_n/n)(R/R_e)^{1/n}$; for $n=4$ and $b_4\simeq7.67$, this is about $-3.4$ at $10R_e$ and continues to steepen, whereas a pure halo power law keeps a fixed slope.

For a <stellar halo> with outer luminosity density proportional to $r^{-p_*}$, the <projection of a spherical power-law emissivity> gives $I_*\propto R^{1-p_*}$. Illustratively, $p_*=3$--$4$ gives projected slopes $-2$--$-3$: then $I_d/I_*\propto R^{p_*-2}$ rises strongly outward, but annihilation can resemble the steeper stellar template. Gas-related gamma emission must be compared with observed gas columns and the <cosmic ray> distribution; it is not always proportional to optical light.

**A shallow, extended, approximately spherical $R^{-1}$ component is particularly distinctive; an $R^{-3}$ component needs additional evidence.** Fit the stellar, gas and halo templates together over a broad radial range, including morphology and emission spectra. A measured slope alone cannot uniquely identify <dark matter>, since stellar or gas profiles can coincide with a halo slope over a limited interval.

= 3
{parent=Paper 65}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $x=m/M_\odot$ and absorb units into the <initial mass function> normalization, $dN=A x^{-3}dx$. The specified <mass-luminosity relation> is $L(x)=L_\odot x^3$, normalized to the Sun. Therefore the total stellar <mass> and <luminosity> are
$$
\frac{M_*}{M_\odot}=A\int_{0.1}^1x^{-2}dx=9A,
\qquad
\frac{L_*}{L_\odot}=A\int_{0.1}^1x^0dx=0.9A.
$$
The unknown normalization cancels. **The mass-to-light ratio is**
$$
\boxed{\Upsilon_* = 10\,\frac{M_\odot}{L_\odot}.}
$$
Low-mass <main-sequence> stars dominate the stellar <mass>, while equal intervals of $x$ contribute equal <luminosity> under these particular exponents. More generally the <mass-to-light ratio of a power-law stellar population> with these exponents on $a<x<b$ is $(M_\odot/L_\odot)/(ab)$.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The result is in the broad range expected for an old, relatively faint <stellar population>, but is high compared with many observed stellar <mass-to-light ratios> of a few solar units in optical bands. The comparison must specify the band and whether the measured <mass> includes <dark matter>: for example, https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0505042[early-type galaxy dynamical measurements] find an $I$-band normalization near $3.8$ solar units at <velocity dispersion> $200\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$, including a contribution from <dark matter>. That is an illustrative observational comparison, not a direct equality with a bolometric stellar calculation.

For an old <stellar population>, an upper surviving <main-sequence> mass near $M_\odot$ is reasonable because more massive stars have shorter lifetimes; low-mass <main-sequence> stars also contain much of the surviving stellar <mass>. However, <red giants> and other evolved stars can contribute a large fraction of the light despite their small numbers, so omitting them generally makes this calculated <mass-to-light ratio> too large. Omitting <stellar remnants> has the opposite effect on the inferred <mass>, and a single cubic <mass-luminosity relation> is only approximate across the stated range. Finally, a single $m^{-3}$ <initial mass function> down to $0.1M_\odot$ is steeper and more low-mass-heavy than commonly adopted distributions, which flatten at low <mass>. **Ten solar units is a useful toy-model estimate, not a precision stellar-population prediction.**

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let the extrapolated lower limit be $a=m_{\min}/M_\odot$ and keep the upper surviving limit at $x=1$. If the same cubic <mass-luminosity relation> is formally continued, the <initial mass function> gives
$$
\frac{M_*}{M_\odot}=A\left(\frac1a-1\right),\qquad
\frac{L_*}{L_\odot}=A(1-a),\qquad
\frac{\Upsilon_*}{M_\odot/L_\odot}=\frac1a.
$$
Matching $1000$ therefore requires **a planetary-scale cutoff**
$$
\boxed{m_{\min}=10^{-3}M_\odot\simeq1M_{\rm Jupiter}.}
$$
The <mass> per logarithmic interval is proportional to $m^{-1}$ for this <initial mass function>, so most of the added <mass> is near the cutoff. Substellar objects do not actually obey the unevolved cubic <mass-luminosity relation>: they cool, and their <luminosity> depends strongly on age. If they are approximated as entirely dark while only the original $0.1$--$1M_\odot$ stars supply light, then $\Upsilon_*=(1/a-1)/0.9$ instead, giving $a=1/901\simeq1.11\times10^{-3}$. Both conventions lead to the same order-of-magnitude conclusion; the exact $10^{-3}$ result uses the question's formal power-law extrapolation.

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Objects near the derived cutoff are too faint for their absence in ordinary star counts alone to exclude a massive <dark-matter halo>. The decisive test is <gravitational microlensing>: compact objects passing in front of background stars produce transient magnification even if they emit no detectable light. For a lens at distance $D_l$ and a source at $D_s$, the physical <Einstein radius> and event timescale are
$$
R_E=\sqrt{\frac{4Gm}{c^2}\frac{D_l(D_s-D_l)}{D_s}},\qquad
 t_E=R_E/v_\perp\propto\sqrt m.
$$
For $D_l=10\,\mathrm{kpc}$, $D_s=50\,\mathrm{kpc}$ and $v_\perp=200\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$, a $10^{-3}M_\odot$ lens gives $t_E\simeq2.2$ days, within the regime tested by suitably sampled <gravitational microlensing> surveys. The <microlensing optical depth> is
$$
\tau=\frac{4\pi G}{c^2}\int_0^{D_s}\rho_l(D_l)\frac{D_l(D_s-D_l)}{D_s}\,dD_l,
$$
so at fixed compact-object <mass density> it is independent of the individual lens <mass>: reducing individual <mass> changes the event durations rather than hiding the total lensing probability.

https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0607207[EROS observations of the Magellanic Clouds] found far fewer candidate events than a halo dominated by compact objects would predict and excluded such objects as the principal Galactic halo component over $6\times10^{-8}<m/M_\odot<15$, under their tested halo models. Their often-quoted eight-percent bound is specifically near $0.4M_\odot$, not a uniform bound at every lens <mass>. **A halo dominated by the approximately Jupiter-mass objects needed here is incompatible with those microlensing constraints.** Deep optical and <infrared> counts also restrict a large population of luminous low-mass stars and <brown dwarfs>, while the <cosmic baryon fraction> provides a separate constraint on explaining all cosmological <dark matter> with baryonic objects.

= v
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

First specify the dynamical estimate: assume an initially isolated virialized system, stars of different <masses> well mixed with the same velocity distribution, and ejecta escaping rapidly compared with a <stellar crossing time>, without imparting kicks to surviving stars. If $f$ is the retained stellar <mass> fraction, the original <virial theorem> gives $2T+W=0$, where $T$ is <kinetic energy> and $W<0$ is <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>. Immediately after loss, positions and velocities are unchanged, so
$$
T'=fT,\qquad W'=f^2W,\qquad
E'=fT+f^2W=|W|f\left(\frac12-f\right).
$$
Thus <impulsive disruption by stellar mass loss> makes the energy of the whole remaining system nonnegative when at least half the initial <mass> is removed.

For the power-law <initial mass function>, the fraction removed is a mass fraction, not a number fraction:
$$
F(\alpha)=\frac{\int_{10}^{100}x^{1-\alpha}dx}{\int_{0.1}^{100}x^{1-\alpha}dx}
=\frac{100^{2-\alpha}-10^{2-\alpha}}{100^{2-\alpha}-0.1^{2-\alpha}}
\quad(\alpha\ne2),\qquad F(2)=\frac{\ln10}{\ln1000}=\frac13.
$$
For integer slopes, $F(0)\simeq0.990$, $F(1)\simeq0.901$, $F(2)=0.333$, and $F(3)\simeq0.009$. Interpolating between $\alpha=1$ and $2$ puts the threshold around $1.7$--$1.8$. Solving $F=1/2$ more accurately gives
$$
2\,10^{2-\alpha}=100^{2-\alpha}+0.1^{2-\alpha},\qquad
\boxed{\alpha_{\rm crit}\simeq1.7910.}
$$
The rearranged equation has a spurious root at $\alpha=2$ introduced when the vanishing denominator was cleared; the logarithmic limit $F(2)=1/3$ excludes it. Increasing $\alpha$ shifts the mass distribution towards lower <masses>, so **the top-heavy side, $\alpha<\alpha_{\rm crit}$, has nonnegative remnant energy in the impulsive model**; equality is marginal. One can see strict monotonicity from the <covariance> obtained by differentiating $F$: $F'=-\operatorname{Cov}(\mathbf1_{x>10},\ln x)<0$ under the normalized mass-weighted distribution.

The <initial mass function> alone does not guarantee complete disruption. If losses occur slowly, the <adiabatic mass-loss expansion law> gives expansion rather than the instantaneous half-mass threshold, and positive total energy can still leave a bound core after selective escape. The stated value is therefore the conventional global impulsive estimate, with no external confining <Newtonian gravitational potential>.

= 4
{parent=Paper 65}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A typical <globular cluster> has undergone appreciable <two-body relaxation>, since its <stellar relaxation time> is $t_{\rm rel}\sim0.1(N/\ln N)t_{\rm cross}$: for $N\sim10^5$ and <stellar crossing time> $t_{\rm cross}\sim10^6\,\mathrm{yr}$ this is about $10^9\,\mathrm{yr}$, shorter than its age, allowing a relatively relaxed, tidally limited structure. A <galaxy> with $N\sim10^{11}$ and $t_{\rm cross}\sim10^8\,\mathrm{yr}$ instead has $t_{\rm rel}\sim4\times10^{16}\,\mathrm{yr}$ and behaves as a <collisionless stellar system>, retaining differences from formation, <galaxy mergers>, and <star formation>. **Relaxation erases more memory in clusters than in galaxies**, although real <globular clusters> also differ in concentration and evolutionary state, so structural similarity is approximate.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At small separations the extended <dark-matter haloes> overlap, and <Chandrasekhar dynamical friction> transfers orbital <energy> to gravitational wakes while tidal interactions also redistribute orbital <energy>. A characteristic decay time scales as $t_{\rm df}\sim v r^2/(Gm\ln\Lambda)$, so sufficiently massive close pairs evolve rapidly towards a <galaxy merger> and spend relatively little time at small separation. **Short residence time depletes the close-pair population**; the quoted separation scale is characteristic of the population, not a universal exclusion radius.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <stellar population> has appreciable random <velocity dispersion>, and its declining radial density creates an outward stress gradient that supplies some support against gravity; cold gas has much less random-motion support and follows nearly the local <circular speed>. Neglecting velocity-ellipsoid tilt, the radial <Jeans equation> gives $v_c^2-\overline v_\phi^2=\sigma_R^2[-d\ln(n_*\sigma_R^2)/d\ln R-1+\sigma_\phi^2/\sigma_R^2]$, generally positive for the stellar disk. **The slower mean stellar rotation is <asymmetric drift>.**

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

A nonrotating <elliptical galaxy> need not have an isotropic <velocity ellipsoid>: unequal random-motion stresses in different directions can support an oblate or triaxial shape without substantial mean rotation. The <tensor virial theorem>, $2T_{ij}+W_{ij}=0$ for a stationary isolated system with appropriate boundary terms, balances this directional kinetic stress against the directional gravitational stress. **Anisotropic random motions can supply the flattening**, whereas an isotropic nonrotating equilibrium has much stronger restrictions on its shape.

= v
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

A <flux-limited galaxy selection> samples a much larger volume for bright <galaxies>: in the nearby Euclidean limit $d_{\max}\propto\sqrt L$, hence $V_{\max}\propto L^{3/2}$, so very numerous faint <dwarf galaxies> are usually detectable only nearby. With a <Schechter function> $\phi(L)\propto L^{\alpha_S}e^{-L/L_*}$, detected counts per logarithmic <luminosity> interval scale as $L^{\alpha_S+5/2}e^{-L/L_*}$ and, for $\alpha_S\sim-1$ to $-1.3$, peak near $L\sim(1.2\text{--}1.5)L_*$ rather than at the numerous faint end. **The competition between increasing survey volume and the bright-end cutoff concentrates detected luminosities near $L_*$**, with the precise width depending on the selection and survey depth.

= vi
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

The differential gravitational field of the <Milky Way> removes loosely bound outer material by <tidal stripping>, giving <globular clusters> and satellite <dwarf galaxies> a characteristic <tidal radius>. For a low-mass satellite of <mass> $m$ on a circular orbit of radius $R$ in a spherical host, the <Jacobi tidal radius> is $r_t\simeq R\{m/[(3-d\ln M_h/d\ln R)M_h(<R)]\}^{1/3}$; eccentric orbits often make the strongest restriction near pericentre. **Tidal limitation explains the sharp outer falloff**, although escaping stars can form extended <tidal tails>, so a fitted luminosity cutoff is not an exact boundary of all associated material.

= vii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vii}

Massive stars rapidly return oxygen, magnesium, neon and other <alpha elements> through <core-collapse supernovae>, whereas <Type Ia supernovae> supply a substantial delayed iron contribution. If the <galactic bulge> and <stellar halo> formed much of their stellar <mass> rapidly, their stars locked in enhanced alpha-element to iron ratios before much <delayed iron enrichment>, while prolonged <star formation> in the local <galactic disk> included more iron-rich gas. **The abundance contrast chiefly records different enrichment timescales**, with the <initial mass function> and gas exchange also affecting the ratios.

= viii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=viii}

In a compact <starburst galaxy>, <stellar feedback> can approach the <radiation-pressure limit of a dusty starburst>, $\kappa F/c\sim g$, so stronger luminosity drives a <galactic outflow> rather than allowing indefinitely increasing <star formation rate>. https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0503027[Optically thick starburst-disk calculations] give a characteristic infrared <flux> near $10^{13}L_\odot\,\mathrm{kpc}^{-2}$; with $L_{\rm IR}\sim10^{10}L_\odot[\dot M_*/(M_\odot\,\mathrm{yr}^{-1})]$ and an emitting area of order $1\,\mathrm{kpc}^2$, this corresponds to $\dot M_*\sim10^3M_\odot\,\mathrm{yr}^{-1}$ and would consume $10^{10}M_\odot$ of gas in only $10^7\,\mathrm{yr}$. **Feedback and a finite fuel supply produce a characteristic upper scale for compact bursts**, rather than a universal ceiling on the total rate independent of star-forming area and gas supply.
